1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,863
When you're unsure when to buy,

2
00:00:01,863 --> 00:00:04,940
people say following buy signals
raises your odds of success.

3
00:00:05,390 --> 00:00:08,509
To check, we ran a
backtest with AI-written

4
00:00:08,509 --> 00:00:10,830
Python code on real past prices.

5
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:16,560
We tested SPY, an ETF that
tracks the S&P 500, from 2016.

6
00:00:17,010 --> 00:00:22,230
We used 10 buy signals, like the 20-day
average, RSI and the fear index VIX.

7
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:27,910
Up 5% from the buy price first counted
as success; down 2% first, as failure.

8
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:31,800
The comparison: 2,000 accounts
that bought on random dates.

9
00:00:32,250 --> 00:00:35,860
If signals are useful, they should
beat random on success rate.

10
00:00:36,310 --> 00:00:38,830
Random accounts were
judged by the same rule.

11
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:44,480
The best was buying when price crosses
the 20-day average: a 52.1% success rate.

12
00:00:44,930 --> 00:00:48,620
But all 10 were in a range no
different from random buying.

13
00:00:49,070 --> 00:00:52,020
So when did each signal
work, and when did it fail?

14
00:00:52,470 --> 00:00:57,010
The 10 signals' records, and whether
other periods agree: see the video below.
